.TH PYDEBUPREP-CLEANUP 8 "November 2026" "pydebuprep 0.9.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pydebuprep\-cleanup \- purge old kernels and leftovers after a Debian or Ubuntu release upgrade
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pydebuprep\-cleanup
.RB [ \-\-kernels ]
.RB [ \-\-modules ]
.RB [ \-\-leftover\-dirs ]
.RB [ \-\-residual ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-keep\ \fIN\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-keep\-package\ \fIPKG\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-apply ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-config\ \fIFILE\fR \ |\  \-\-no\-config ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pydebuprep\-cleanup
purges what a release upgrade leaves behind: old kernels, their modules and
headers, directories DKMS leaves in them, and the configuration of removed
packages. Unlike
.BR pydebuprep (1),
it changes the system, so it only shows what it would do unless it is run with
.BR \-\-apply .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-kernels
Old kernel images (linux\-image\-\fIVERSION\fR\-\fIFLAVOUR\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-modules
Modules, headers and tools of old kernels, including those whose image is
already gone.
.TP
.B \-\-leftover\-dirs
Directories of old kernels under /lib/modules and /usr/src that no package owns
(or only packages this run purges), even when they aren't empty.
.TP
.B \-\-residual
The configuration left by removed packages (dpkg state rc). Their configuration
files are backed up first.
.TP
.B \-\-all
All of the above.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " N"
Kernels to keep, besides the running one and GRUB's default (1 to 10, default 2).
.TP
.BI \-\-keep\-package " PKG"
Never purge this package; may be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Carry out the plan. Without it nothing changes.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Don't ask before acting; needed when not at a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the plan as JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read
.B cleanup.keep_packages
from this config file instead of /etc/pydebuprep.conf.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Ignore any config file.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show the options and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH GUARDRAILS
The running kernel, the newest installed image, the newest \fIN\fR images and
every kernel GRUB is set to boot (GRUB_DEFAULT in /etc/default/grub and
grub.d, and grubenv's saved and next entries) are always kept, as are meta
packages (linux\-image\-generic, linux\-image\-amd64) and held packages. If
the running kernel can't be identified, or GRUB_DEFAULT names an entry that
can't be matched to a kernel, no kernel, module or directory is removed. A
kernel's directories are removed only when no package of that kernel stays
installed. Before acting it refuses unless run as root, while apt or dpkg is
running, when
.B dpkg \-\-audit
reports problems or can't run, or while another cleanup runs. It plans under
its lock, asks apt to simulate the purge, and refuses if apt would remove or
install anything not in the plan; each purge is simulated again just before it
runs. A failed step, or Ctrl\-C (after apt and dpkg finish what they started),
stops the run with status 2.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/pydebuprep/cleanup.log
Every run's plan and every action; also in the journal:
.BR "journalctl \-t pydebuprep\-cleanup" .
.TP
.I /var/backups/pydebuprep
The configuration files of purged rc packages, as rc\-\fITIMESTAMP\fR.tar.gz.
.TP
.I /etc/pydebuprep.conf
.B cleanup: keep_packages:
lists packages to leave alone. A config fetched from a portal can't set it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 done, or nothing to do; 1 refused by a guardrail; 2 a step failed (the rest
was left undone); 3 a usage or config error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pydebuprep (1),
.BR apt\-get (8),
.BR dpkg (1)
